{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Korah took, etc.</b> One needs to know what it means by saying <b>and he took</b>, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 18) that he took himself to one side, and in this reading the word ״עצמו״ (himself) is missing — for the mention of Korah is to say who is the one who took, and missing is what was taken. And if his intention is to say as Onkelos renders ״ואתפליג״ (and he split), the measure of the word ״ואתפליג״ does not equal the measure of the word ״ויקח,״ but the Targum explains what is intended. And although their words are true, there is yet further for the Sage to examine the word ״ויקח״ precisely. (b) There is a point to raise about a true matter: why does HaShem mention the memory of the righteous — Yitzhar, Kehat, Levi — in an unfitting matter such as this, and what need is there in it? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (ibid.) that Jacob prayed on his own behalf that his name not be remembered in this matter — implying that it needed to be mentioned, except that he prayed. And I marvel also at those whom He did mention. (g) One needs to know the intention of the verse in the statement <b>and Datan and Aviram</b> — what is the verse's intention in mentioning them, what are their deeds? If to say that they too took themselves, it should have included them in the taking and said ״ויקחו קרח וגו׳ ודתן ואבירם,״ and if his intention is to say that Korah took them, it should have said ״ויקח קרח וגו׳ דתן ואבירם״ etc. (d) The saying <b>and they rose up</b> before Moshe — what does the verse tell in this rising? If to say that they came to dispute, after that it says ״ויקהלו וגו׳ ויאמרו״ etc., and if to say that they stood with upright posture before him, it should have said ״ויקומו״ after ״ויקהלו״ in this manner: ״ויקהלו וגו׳ ויקומו וגו׳ ויאמרו״ etc. And why did it interrupt between ״ויקומו״ and ״ויקהלו״ with the statement ״and men of the children of Israel״ etc.?" "<b>Truly,</b> the verse intended to inform the foundation of the dispute — that a person should ask: from where did it arise upon the mind of Korah to dispute a faithful prophet whose prophecies were proven true? And Korah was a great sage, so how was he seduced to do this thing? Would not all Israel stone him? Therefore it states the reasons that he took into his mind to come to contend. First — <b>son of Yitzhar</b>, meaning, his being a son of Yitzhar, who is greater than the two brothers who come after him among the sons of Kehat, and they cannot brazen their faces against him, for he is greater than them. Second — <b>son of Kehat</b>, he is the most distinguished of the sons of Levi, who carried the holy of holies, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 13a) that Korah was among the bearers of the Ark, and Gershon and Merari will not speak against him. <b>Son of Levi</b> — that he is a tribe distinguished above all Israel, and the rest of the tribes will not cut him down when he speaks before Moshe, for he is distinguished above all the rest of the tribes, and above all the sons of Levi, and also among the branches of Kehat he is in the first rank relative to the two brothers who come after him, who are Chevron and Uzziel." "And the saying <b>and Datan and Aviram</b> etc., meaning — them too he took in order to challenge also with their power, for they are the princes of the tribe of Reuven, the firstborn of Jacob, and through this mediation they elevated themselves. <b>And they rose up before Moshe</b>, meaning, a rising and elevation on a par with Moshe — Korah for what he represents as mentioned, and Datan and Aviram on account of their being princes of Reuven, who is greater than all the brothers and also than Levi. And the saying <b>and men</b> etc., meaning — these too he took with him on account of their being princes of Israel, in order to strengthen his arms; but these did not come in this category to rise up before Moshe, but rather they assembled to uphold the words of Korah and his faction. And therefore it preceded to say ״ויקומו״ before the mention of the two hundred and fifty etc., and with this all the fine points are resolved." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may be explained by prefacing their words — their memory for a blessing (Numbers Rabbah 18:2) — where they said that the core of Korah's dispute was against Elitzafan son of Uzziel, to whom Moshe had given the leadership. And the verse informed that Korah took a line of contradictions in Moshe's actions. First, the claim of his being a son of Yitzhar — from either side: if you follow after the greatest of the sons, and therefore you gave the priesthood to Aharon on account of his being a son of Amram, if so, by that very reasoning the leadership befits me, on account of my being a son of Yitzhar; second to Amram, who is a son of Kehat — which is the branch itself from which Aharon came, preceding Chevron and Uzziel — so why did you give the leadership to Elitzafan? Behold, your actions prove that there is no precedence for the greatest of the sons; if so, the reasoning returns to why he gave the priesthood to Aharon on the ground that Amram is the firstborn — this reasoning is no reasoning. And from this point, since they found the words of Moshe contradictory, if so he enters the category of falsehood, heaven forfend, and also in the priesthood of Aharon his words will not be trusted to follow after the precedence of Amram; rather, the entire tribe of Levi is equal, and this is what he alluded to in the statement ״son of Levi.״" "And on the side that Korah feared that his claims would not be accepted except to nullify the leadership of Elitzafan, but to say that one should not be concerned with the precedence of Amram over Yitzhar — no one would listen to him, for it is known from this that the elder takes precedence in rank. Therefore he encompassed with the claim of Reuven the firstborn over all the tribes, and took the princes of Reuven — and this is the statement <b>Datan and Aviram</b> etc. <b>sons of Reuven</b>, to say: if you compel me to say, let us follow the greatest — let us follow the firstborn of all the brothers, and to him let the priesthood be given. And the saying <b>and they rose up before Moshe</b>, meaning to arrange a line of contradictions to overturn his words. And the saying <b>and men of the children of Israel</b> etc. — he brought them in order to set before them the proofs of his contention, so that they would not reject him by force of authority." "<b>And for our Sages of blessed memory</b> I have seen that they said (Sanhedrin 109b) the reason the verse mentions the memory of those who begot him, and this is their language: ״קֹרַח״ — that he made a bald spot (קָרְחָה) in Israel; ״בֶּן יִצְהָר״ — that he heated (הִרְתִּיחַ) the world like the noon heat (צָּהֳרַיִם); ״בֶּן קְהָת״ — that he blunt (קִהָה) the teeth of those who begot him; ״בֶּן לֵוִי״ — that he became attached (נַעֲשָׂה לְוָיָה) to Gehenna — and why not also count ״בֶּן יַעֲקֹב,״ that he dragged (עָקַב) himself to Gehenna? Jacob sought mercy, etc., end quote. And it seems to me a wonder why one would designate evil things by the names of the righteous, and not only this, but the author of the homily further asks ״and why not count 'son of Jacob'״ etc. — which is not fitting, to mention the name of the choicest of the Patriarchs in such a manner. There is further cause to note their saying that ״he dragged himself to Gehenna,״ for he has already said ״son of Levi״ — that he attached himself to Gehenna — so why repeat the matter twice?" "<b>And to illuminate</b> the light of Torah I shall arrange two or three morsels from the hidden recesses of Torah. The first: that the branches of holiness which the Emanator, blessed be He, prepared and arranged — these are the pathways and ordinances of Torah which HaShem ordained through Moses His servant. And whoever changes the order from its arrangement — behold, he uproots that branch in the sod aspect of his soul, and renders that branch an evil branch, for the sanctifying force withdraws from it. And this is your sign: the sod of the sacrifice that becomes disqualified and is deemed impure by a thought of outside its time and outside its place, and in leftover meat (notar), descending from the glory of its holiness to become impure and rendering those who eat it liable to excision (karet)." "Second: that the very branch through which one wrought iniquity itself precedes to send its hand against him, in the sod of (Jeremiah 2:19) ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״ (your wickedness shall chastise you). Thus you find that the branch of holiness is turned to evil, and its very glory becomes the destroyer, may the Merciful One spare us." "Third. Know that the aspect of the souls which HaShem planted in His people — they are the letters of Torah, as is known to those who know grace (חֵן):" "Fourth) Know that when HaShem created man, He created one planting in which all the branches of holiness were included. And when he sinned and was blemished, all the souls that depended on it were blemished, and all that issued from it were blemished — until the soul of Abraham our father came forth, and it was refined through the mediation of the ten trials, and the dross was separated out in Ishmael. And the soul of Isaac came forth and was refined at the Binding (Akedah), and the dross was separated out through the predominance of fire, namely Esau. And the soul of Jacob came forth without any flaw. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory admonished us in their saying (Bava Metzia 84a): ״The beauty of Jacob our father resembled the beauty of Adam the first,״ end quote — meaning, there was no dross in him, just as in Adam before he sinned." "<b>We find ourselves saying</b> that the tree is Jacob, and from it emerged twelve branches, and one of them is Levi. This branch put forth three branches: Gershon, Kehat, and Merari, and Kehat put forth four branches: Amram, Yitzhar, Hevron, and Uzziel, and the branch of Yitzhar is Korah. Now, when Korah came to exchange the branches of holiness and to overturn the priesthood from its place, the branches of holiness in the root of his soul — all of which had been for praise — were damaged, and it began from below upward. First, the branch itself, which is Korah: in the beginning he was called Korah tahor (pure), ״בֶּן יִצְהָר״ — that he illuminated the world like the noonday; ״בֶּן קְהָת״ — that he blunted (memaheh) the teeth of all who beheld his greatness and virtue; ״בֶּן לֵוִי״ — that he attached himself from the day of his birth to the Holy One, blessed be He, in many degrees. And now the branch itself that is called Korah was damaged and made a bald spot (korḥah) in it; and not his branch alone, but also the root of the branch that comes from him, which is Yitzhar, turned to evil in the root of his soul and heated the entire world like the noonday. And not one root alone, but also the root above it, which is בֶּן קְהָת, turned to evil to blunt (lehaḥkot) the teeth of his progenitors. And not this alone, but also the root of the first branch that emerged from Jacob — the root of the soul of Korah, which there was uprooted — was damaged and took vengeance upon it to escort it to Gehenna. And from the fact that the verse did not mention ״בֶּן יַעֲקֹב״, the first root, which is the very trunk of the tree, which is Jacob — it is not yet known whether his deeds were recognized, that is, whether the damage also reached there, and it turns out that the root of Korah was uprooted from its very foundation, or not." "<b>And know</b> that there is a dispute among our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 109b) regarding Korah and his assembly, whether they have a portion in the World to Come. According to the one who says he has no portion in the World to Come, his root in Jacob was also uprooted, and this is what the questioner asked: ״וְלִחְשׁוּב בֶּן יַעֲקֹב״, and he examined closely to say that עָקַב (eikev) itself — to Gehenna, meaning not attached for a brief time only, but until the end of the world. And this is what he alluded to in the expression עָקַב עַצְמוֹ — a term of end and conclusion, like the heels (akeivim), which are the extremity of a person's structure. And our Rabbis of blessed memory use this expression, as they said (Sotah 49b): ״בְּעִקְּבוֹת מְשִׁיחָא״, etc. And he answers: because he prayed for himself, etc. — meaning, it is indeed so, except that HaShem gave satisfaction of spirit to Jacob that he prayed, etc. Or it is possible that he holds he does have a portion in the World to Come, and this was Jacob's prayer — that the power of the sin not reach the very root that is in Jacob, and that the silver cord not be severed from its foundation, and there be hope for their end. And this is the statement of the Tanna (Sanhedrin ibid.): concerning them the verse said (I Samuel 2:6) ״מוֹרִיד שְׁאוֹל וַיָּעַל״. And I saw fit to examine the verse's saying ״וַיָּעַל״ and not ״וּמַעֲלֶה״ — as would be expected by analogy with what it says nearby, ״YHWH מֵמִית וּמְחַיֶּה״, and likewise it says afterward, ״YHWH מוֹרִישׁ וּמַעֲשִׁיר״. However, the intention was to say the very words of the Tanna himself — that it is about the assembly of Korah that he speaks; therefore he said ״וַיָּעַל״ — meaning, He had already elevated them before He brought them down, for the prayer of Jacob preceded their descent, and from then on they were inscribed in the book of ascent."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {10 "The wordplay on קֹרַח (Korah) / קָרְחָה (bald spot) and יִצְהָר / צָהֳרַיִם (noonday) and קְהָת / מַקְהֶה (blunts) is central and rendered with transliterated Hebrew retained to keep the etymological argument visible."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:25.519955419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}